.venv/
# The presentation build environment, created from
# presentation/requirements-presentation.txt. A virtualenv is a build
# product, not a record of one.
.venv-presentation/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
data/raw/
data/processed/
artifacts/checkpoints/
artifacts/renders/
.DS_Store
.hf_cache/

# Local credentials and editor state
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.idea/
.vscode/

# Model artifacts must be reviewed explicitly before redistribution
*.safetensors
*.ckpt
*.pth
*.pt

# Session locks are per-machine runtime state, not results. Matched anywhere
# under data/reports/ -- report 16 names its lock `.lock` inside the session
# directory rather than `.<id>.lock` beside it, so a rule written for report
# 15's shape left exp001/.lock untracked-but-not-ignored and one `git add`
# away from being committed.
data/reports/**/.lock
data/reports/**/.*.lock

# Per-record evidence must never reach a public export. These paths stay in
# the private research tree: they are session journals, launch records,
# watchdog logs, source snapshots and failure evidence, and the frozen split
# manifest, which is a per-object identifier list.
#
# They are deliberately *not* ignored here -- the private repository has no
# remote and records them on purpose. The public export is built by an
# explicit allowlist (scripts/17_public_snapshot.py), never by mirroring this
# working tree, so this block is documentation of the boundary rather than a
# rule the private tree enforces on itself.
#   data/reports/15_mps_order/exp*/
#   data/reports/16_longrun/
#   data/splits/

# Gate run output: ledgers, checkpoints, optimizer state and saved adapters.
# Per-machine runtime state, and on the execution node it lives inside the
# training pack rather than in a repository at all. Ignored here for the same
# reason the session locks above are: on this machine it can only ever be
# self-test scratch, and one `git add -A` away from being committed.
runs/

# The materialised core-acceptance plan. It is derived from the test split --
# 160 captions and inventories -- so it is per-record content and belongs
# outside version control for the same reason data/processed/ does. It is
# nonetheless on the private *pack* allowlist on purpose: the execution node
# cannot generate without the prompts, and putting it anywhere the pack
# verifier ignores (runs/) would make it swappable after the pack was audited.
gpu_plans/

# Third-party runtimes. The V2 PBR track pins a Blender install by digest;
# the installer itself is a ~400 MB third-party binary, so the *record* of it
# (version, DMG SHA-256, the official checksum file and where it came from) is
# tracked and the binary is not. Kept on disk rather than re-downloaded so a
# replay does not depend on a release directory staying up.
artifacts/runtime/

# Never, in either tree
*.gguf
*.h5
*.msgpack
.hf_home/

# BrickNet extension: the derived corpora, not the records of them.
#
# `pt_sample.jsonl` is 907 MB and `train.jsonl` 59 MB, both derived from the
# author's gated dataset -- per-record content, excluded for the same reason
# `data/processed/` is. Their manifests, which carry the SHA-256, the selection
# rule and the row counts, *are* tracked: the record of a dataset is what makes
# it checkable, and it is small.
data/bricknet/instruct/*.jsonl
data/bricknet/instruct_all/*.jsonl
data/bricknet/pt/*.jsonl
data/bricknet/catalog/*.jsonl

# Transport packs for the Taichung node. Build outputs, not evidence: their
# bytes are reproducible from the dataset plus the pack_digest, and the data
# pack carries derivatives of the author's gated dataset, which do not go into
# Git (CLAUDE.md, decision 5). The *frozen* packs that are evidence --
# artifacts/bricknet/evalpack_frozen and the evidence_* archives -- stay
# tracked, so this is deliberately narrow.
artifacts/bricknet/pack_scale_code/
artifacts/bricknet/pack_scale_data/
artifacts/bricknet/product_node_pack_v1/
artifacts/bricknet/product_node_pack_v2/
artifacts/bricknet/product_node_pack_v3/
artifacts/bricknet/product_node_pack_v4/
artifacts/bricknet/product_return_v3/
artifacts/bricknet/product_diagnostic_v3/brickagain_generated_taichung.json

# Per-slide renders of presentation/deck/brickagain_showcase.pptx. They are a
# check, not a deliverable: `presentation/tools/render_deck.py` rebuilds them
# from the .pptx in seconds, and render_manifest.json keeps the result of the
# check that used them. 4 MB of bitmaps that a command regenerates is not
# evidence worth versioning.
presentation/deck/render/

# Local recovery copies and private audit scratch files, never publish.
artifacts/codex-backup-20260907/

# Local upstream installation; never include in a product/source commit.
/BrickNet-master/

# Editable local package metadata
/brickagain_local.egg-info/

# Photo-recognition track: the payload, not the record of it.
#
# `data/bricknet/photo/shards/` holds the rendered PNG corpus -- about 14.6 GiB
# at full scope -- which is derived content in the same sense `data/processed/`
# is. Each shard's `manifest.json` and `labels.jsonl` are what make it
# checkable and are small, so they are excluded from this rule below.
#
# `artifacts/bricknet/photo/checkpoints/` holds what the node *trains*. Its
# weights are already caught by `*.safetensors`, but the directory is named so
# that an optimizer state file or a tokenizer config landing beside them is not
# one `git add -A` from being committed.
#
# `artifacts/bricknet/photo/weights/` holds the two fetched third-party
# backbones. Same treatment as artifacts/runtime/: the *record* of them --
# repository, revision, licence and per-file SHA-256 in
# data/reports/bricknet/photo/01_weights.json -- is tracked, and the bytes,
# including their config.json, are not.
# Written as `/**` with the directories re-included rather than as
# `shards/`: git does not descend into an excluded *directory*, so a negation
# for a file inside one never gets a chance to match. Checked, not assumed --
# the first spelling ignored manifest.json as well.
data/bricknet/photo/shards/**
!data/bricknet/photo/shards/
!data/bricknet/photo/shards/*/
!data/bricknet/photo/shards/*/manifest.json
!data/bricknet/photo/shards/*/labels.jsonl
# The OptiX corpus, under its own directory because the one above is retired
# evidence and may not be rendered into. Its retirement notice is small and
# is the record of that decision, so it is tracked.
!data/bricknet/photo/shards/SUPERSEDED.json
data/bricknet/photo/shards_optix/**
!data/bricknet/photo/shards_optix/
!data/bricknet/photo/shards_optix/*/
!data/bricknet/photo/shards_optix/*/manifest.json
!data/bricknet/photo/shards_optix/*/labels.jsonl
artifacts/bricknet/photo/checkpoints/
artifacts/bricknet/photo/weights/
